            "content": "Layer 3 of the reasoning-effort stack. Config surface only — nothing consumes\nthe declaration yet.\n\nReplaces the `models[].reasoning` display boolean with an object declaring\nwhich reasoning-effort values a model accepts:\n\n```jsonc\n\"reasoning\": {\n  \"effortLevels\": [\"none\", \"low\", \"medium\", \"high\", \"xhigh\", \"max\"],\n  \"defaultEffort\": \"medium\",\n  \"cacheInvalidatedByEffortChange\": true\n}\n```\n\nPresence is the declaration — a model that omits it accepts no effort — so\nthere is no separate availability flag to keep in sync with the level list.\n\n## Why the levels are unvalidated strings\n\n`effortLevels` are the provider's own tokens, resolved and published verbatim:\nno enum, no character pattern, no casing rule, and no normalizing, sorting,\ntrimming, or deduplication of the text.\n\nThat is the whole point rather than an omission. OpenAI currently documents\n`none | low | medium | high | xhigh | max`; `@ai-sdk/openai`'s own enum still\ncarries the retired `minimal` and only gained `max` in 3.0.97. Anthropic's\n`effort` is `low | medium | high | xhigh | max` — no `minimal`, no `none`. Any\nconstraint encoded here would encode what today's vocabularies happen to look\nlike, and turn the next provider release into a llame release. A regex is the\nsame mistake wearing a different hat.\n\nThree constraints survive, all integrity rather than format: a level is\nnonblank, levels are unique within an entry, and `defaultEffort` must be one of\nthem. The first two are JSON Schema (`minLength`, `uniqueItems`); the third is\ncross-field and inexpressible there, so `resolveModels` owns it and fails boot\nnaming the model, the default, and the declared levels.\n\nByte-exact matching then needs no casing rule of its own — a `defaultEffort` of\n`\"High\"` against a declared `\"high\"` is simply not a match, and fails boot\nrather than being silently folded.\n\n## Breaking\n\n`reasoning: true` is **rejected, not coerced**. A